From 38e38113ea14cb5f160fea493990bfb44d51373a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lakr <git@qaq.wiki>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 16:44:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 41/45] Answer a heap size query before its doorbell returns

A pulled macOS 27.2 (26B123) showed grey placeholders for every Dock
icon. In the guest, iconservicesagent and com.apple.dock.extra logged
RenderBox's "unable to create heap: 0" hundreds of times a boot, and
then "Failed to generate flatten representation for icon stack".

RenderBox sizes a texture heap from
heapTextureSizeAndAlignWithDescriptor:. A probe run in the guest made
that call 40 times: 27 returned {0, 0}, each in 10 to 60 us, and the 13
that returned a size took 1 to 27 ms. The host log had answered all 40
correctly. The guest sends CmdHeapTextureSizeAndAlign, rings the child
doorbell and reads the sixteen reply bytes as soon as the write returns;
it waits for no stamp and no interrupt (each query carries a fresh
completion stamp that nothing waits on). Apple's host answers inside the
doorbell write. This device hands the doorbell to the drain worker, so
the guest read its own zeros unless the vCPU happened to lose the race.

- The vCPU that rings a child doorbell looks through that channel's
  ring, with no device lock, for heap size queries
  (runtime::reply_fence). Nearly every doorbell finds none and returns
  after three register reads.
- When it finds one it takes the device lock, waiting for the drain's
  tranche to end at most 250 ms, and writes the reply itself. The query
  changes nothing in the device, so it need not wait its turn: a channel
  held behind a shader translation kept the first version of this, which
  waited for the drain to reach the packet, at its timeout 147 times in
  one boot.
- The drain leaves an answered packet's reply alone when it reaches it;
  the guest may have reused those bytes by then.
- A query that cannot be answered here, its reply address not mapped
  yet, waits for the drain to pass it, as before bounded.

After it the probe read a size from all 40 calls, and neither process
logged the heap error again in a boot.

Tests (lib, --test-threads=1, at the end of this series): 2317 passed,
0 failed. New: the ring walk finds a
query behind another packet with its payload, finds none in a ring
without one, reads one that wraps the ring, stops at an unpublished
packet, and reports the ring drained only once the head passes the
query.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
